  "id": "unique-paths",
  "questionId": "unique-paths",
  "slug": "unique-paths",
  "title": "Unique Paths",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Dp 2d",
  "patterns": [
    "dynamic programming",
    "grid",
    "combinatorics"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "blind-75",
    "grind-75"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "the robot can only arrive at cell (i, j) from two places - which ones?",
    "define dp[i][j] as the number of distinct paths from the start to cell (i, j)",
    "what's the recurrence: dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j] + dp[i][j-1], since a path reaches (i, j) either from above or from the left",
    "the first row and first column each only have one possible path (all moves in one direction), so they're your base case, all set to 1",
    "fill the table row by row and return dp[m-1][n-1]"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "off-by-one errors when initializing the first row and first column",
    "trying to solve it with plain recursion without memoization, which blows up exponentially for larger m, n",
    "confusing m (rows) and n (columns) when indexing the grid"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A robot starts in the top-left corner of an `m` x `n` grid. It can only move either right or down at each step, and it wants to reach the bottom-right corner.\n\n**Given the grid dimensions `m` and `n`, return the number of distinct paths the robot can take to reach its destination.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= m <= 100",
      "1 <= n <= 100",
      "the answer is guaranteed to be less than or equal to 2 * 10^9"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "m = 1 or n = 1 (a single row or single column, only one path exists)",
      "m = 1 and n = 1 (already at the destination)",
      "a square grid where m = n"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "m = 3, n = 7",
        "output": "28",
        "expectedOutput": "28",
        "explanation": ""
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "m = 3, n = 2",
        "output": "3",
        "expectedOutput": "3",
        "explanation": "From the top-left, the paths are: right,down,down / down,right,down / down,down,right"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "m = 1, n = 1",
        "output": "1",
        "expectedOutput": "1",
        "explanation": "The robot is already at the destination, so there's exactly one (empty) path."
      }
    ],
